00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:31,060
So Laura King was a student of Penny Baker, took the exact opposite approach and said,

122
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:38,380
let's study what happens when the individuals write about their most intense positive experience.

123
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:46,580
And what she did specifically was take the instructions from Abraham Maslow's work on peak experience. And here are the instructions.

124
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:52,480
Once again, this was three times 15 minutes. so 45 minutes total on three consecutive days.

125
00:15:53,320 --> 00:16:00,700
Think of the most wonderful experience or experiences in your life. Happiest moments, ecstatic moments, moments of rapture,

126
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:09,300
perhaps from being in love or from listening to music or suddenly being hit by a book or painting or from some great creative moment.

127
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:20,240
Choose one such experience or moment. Try to imagine yourself at that moment, including all the feelings and emotions associated with the experience.

128
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:30,620
Now, write about the experience in as much detail as possible, trying to include the feelings, thoughts, and emotions that were present at the time.

129
00:16:30,920 --> 00:16:35,020
Please try your best to re-experience the emotions involved.

130
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:38,940
Essentially the exact opposite of Penny Baker.

131
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:43,300
Results? Identical.

132
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:53,020
Those who wrote about their peak experiences, their best experiences, visited the doctor's office last time.

133
00:16:53,060 --> 00:17:01,640
In other words, it strengthened their physiological immune system and experienced more happiness, both psychological and physiological results.

134
00:17:01,860 --> 00:17:12,780
Now, when you look at this, some of you may be thinking back, well, what about the Lubomirsky study? Remember the Lubomirsky study that said when you write about positive emotions,

135
00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:14,200
actually you feel worse.

136
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:20,440
Whereas when you write about negative emotions, you do feel better, as Penny Baker suggested.

137
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:22,840
Here is the difference.

138
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:27,460
The instructions that Laura King gave

139
00:17:28,380 --> 00:17:38,140
were mostly about describing and re-experiencing, replaying your experience. It wasn't about analyzing why did it happen,

140
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:42,760
how did it, it was rather how it happened. In other words, replaying it.

141
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:55,720
When the instructions, and this is the Lubomirsky study, were analyzed the experience, why it happened, how did you get there, and so on and so on, that's when it had the negative consequences over time.

142
00:17:55,900 --> 00:18:01,460
But just replaying the experience, just writing about how wonderful it is,

143
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:07,620
re-experiencing emotionally what that was, that actually led to benefits.

144
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:15,980
Now, the interesting question is, why? Why are there such remarkable benefits to journaling?

145
00:18:16,820 --> 00:18:18,740
Let's understand the process, the mechanism.

146
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:29,040
So there are a few things that are at play here. One of the things, for example, with positive emotions is that when you're replaying it, you're simply fortifying the neural pathways.

147
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:35,400
You replay it, you imagine it again, and you make it more likely to happen again.

148
00:18:36,060 --> 00:18:46,860
Just like when you have a river, remember the analogy from two weeks ago, you have a river, and the more water goes through it, the wider it becomes, and then the more water is likely to go into it.

149
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:49,720
That's the self-reinforcing nature of change.

150
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:56,420
So the first thing, the first reason why writing is especially about painful emotions, but not just painful emotions,

151
00:18:56,780 --> 00:18:59,140
the reason why it helps is because of tension.

152
00:18:59,540 --> 00:19:04,240
We talked about ironic processing, Daniel Wagner's work from here.

153
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:16,860
And what he talks about is that when we suppress a natural phenomenon, it often intensifies, be it thinking about a pink elephant, or be it suppressing painful emotions.

154
00:19:16,860 --> 00:19:22,900
And when we give ourselves the permission to be human, we're much more likely to release it, to let go,

155
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:30,500
which is why therapy helps, which is why sharing with friends, opening up helps, which is why journaling helps.

156
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:33,420
This is the whole idea of suppression or repression.

157
00:19:36,390 --> 00:19:44,650
The second thing that Penny Baker talks about is coherence. One of the things that he finds is that the individuals who benefit most,

158
00:19:45,290 --> 00:19:49,690
and he analyzes the text confidentially, of course, but he analyzes the text,

159
00:19:50,370 --> 00:20:01,550
the individuals who benefit most from the study are ones who introduced a lot of insight words or insight phrases, meaning by the third day they're already writing,

160
00:20:01,550 --> 00:20:10,310
now I see that, or I understand that, or it just dawned on me that, or I realize that.

161
00:20:10,550 --> 00:20:20,670
Those who have many of these words or phrases in their writings are the ones who benefited most. In other words, they've created a sense of coherence out of the experience.

162
00:20:20,830 --> 00:20:24,950
They made sense out of something that perhaps before that was senseless.

163
00:20:26,190 --> 00:20:36,410
Or in other words, they created a story around their experiences. It's no longer disparate, disjointed, disconnected data.

164
00:20:37,510 --> 00:20:41,570
Now it is a coherent whole. It's a story. Now I can deal with it.

165
00:20:42,370 --> 00:20:50,850
And if you think about it, what do people mostly remember? People mostly remember stories. Why? Because you remember a story because it's one unit. It's a whole.

166
00:20:52,170 --> 00:21:00,950
If I'd given you now a collection of, you know, 100 random words, it would take you a very long time to commit it to memory.

167
00:21:01,870 --> 00:21:03,970
I mean, we all know, we all took the SATs.

168
00:21:05,750 --> 00:21:17,490
But if I tell you a story, you're much more likely to remember it. Maybe not word for word, but certainly the general ideas. Because we can hold it, we can grasp it, because it has a sense of coherence.

169
00:21:17,950 --> 00:21:23,690
I can deal with it, I can handle it. As opposed to some disparate ideas, disjointed words.

170
00:21:24,150 --> 00:21:30,490
The exact same here. We want to feel that our lives have a sense of coherence, that we can make sense of them.

171
00:21:31,250 --> 00:21:31,870
Penny Baker.

172
00:21:32,830 --> 00:21:43,670
An artifact of our ambiguous and unpredictable world is the anxiety of not attaining completion and not understanding a simple cause and effect explanation for traumatic disturbances.

173
00:21:44,370 --> 00:21:52,930
Alas, we naturally search for meaning in the completion of events. It gives us a sense of control and predictability over our lives.

174
00:21:53,350 --> 00:22:02,150
Again, that's why therapy helps, because we create coherence out of experiences. This is why there was far less post-traumatic stress disorder after the Holocaust

175
00:22:02,150 --> 00:22:10,110
than there was after the Vietnam War. Because after the Holocaust, they spoke about these experiences.

176
00:22:10,750 --> 00:22:15,290
They created a story around it, a tragic one, but still there was a sense of coherence.

177
00:22:15,810 --> 00:22:25,090
As opposed to Vietnam when it was just random flashes of experiences without giving themselves the permission or without society in many ways

178
00:22:25,090 --> 00:22:32,350
giving them the permission to open up and to create a story out of it that they could handle, that they could grasp.

179
00:22:34,930 --> 00:22:45,090
The work of Penny Baker, to a great extent, relies on the work of a psychologist whom we've mentioned on the very first day of class, and that is Aaron Antonovsky.

180
00:22:47,010 --> 00:22:53,410
Aaron Antonovsky, whom I consider to be one of the fathers of positive psychology,

181
00:22:53,410 --> 00:23:06,390
If you remember, came up with the notion of salutogenesis, an alternative to the pathogenic model, pathogenic model, the model that focuses on pathology, on sickness and illness.

182
00:23:07,150 --> 00:23:14,870
Whereas what Antonovsky said is we need to focus on the origins of health, salutogenesis origin.

183
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:27,940
And what he did specifically was the following. So he was a sociologist and he said the following. And he said, look, life is tough. People go through hardship. They go through difficulties.

184
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:32,860
Whether we like it or not, we all in life encounter hardships.

185
00:23:32,860 --> 00:23:43,640
In a relationship, in school, at work, wherever, whichever domain, there are hardships, there are difficulties. Life is hard, period. We know that.

186
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:57,540
However, there are some people who are able to better deal with the hardship, with the difficulties, who still, despite, they don't do away with these hardships, despite these hardships and difficulties,

187
00:23:57,720 --> 00:24:02,260
are still able to lead a full, fulfilling, and overall happy life.

